_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 23
---
order: 23
---
short_label: III.23
---
statement: On the same straight line there cannot be constructed two similar and unequal segments of circles on the same side.
---
proof:

For, if possible, on the same straight line *AB* let two similar and unequal segments of circles *ACB* and *ADB* be constructed on the same side.  Draw *ACD* through, and join *CB* and *DB.*

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="250" height="130" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.23 diagram" src="propIII23.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.23",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;40,100",
            "B;point;free;210,100",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "C;point;free;115,55",
            "BCA;sector;arc;B,C,A;0;0;black;0",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C",
            "D;point;lineSlider;A,C,150,30",
            "BDA;sector;arc;B,D,A;0;0;black;0",
            "AD;line;connect;A,D"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Then, since the segment *ACB* is similar to the segment *ADB,* and similar segments of circles are those which admit equal angles, the angle *ACB* equals the angle *ADB,* the exterior to the interior, which is impossible. [!just III.Def.11; I.16]

Therefore *on the same straight line there cannot be constructed two similar and unequal segments of circles on the same side.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
<br clear="all">
---
body:

This proposition is used in the next one.
